  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Accenture Federal Services has $2.8B in contract obligations in FY2026 to date, spread across 375 awards with an average award size of $7.5M. Accenture Federal Services obligations are down 13.2% year over year. Accenture Federal Services received larger obligations in prior fiscal years: $2.5B in FY21, $2.7B in FY22, $2.9B in FY23, $3.4B in FY24, and $3.2B in FY25. Accenture Federal Services work in FY2026 to date is concentrated in Computer Systems Design Services, which accounts for $2.5B of obligations. Accenture Federal Services also serves a broad set of agencies: the Department of Defense obligated $486M, the Department of Energy obligated $378M, and the Department of Education obligated $340M, among others. This profile shows a government business that remains large and diversified across agencies and IT services, even as obligations are lower than the prior fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Accenture Federal Services obligations by market?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services is the largest market for Accenture Federal Services, with $2.5B in obligations in FY2026 to date. Accenture Federal Services has smaller obligations in Other Computer Related Services ($126M), Custom Computer Programming Services ($92.6M), and All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services ($45.2M). The remaining listed markets each represent single-digit millions in obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive Accenture Federal Services obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer from Accenture Federal Services with $486M in obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Energy ($378M), the Department of Education ($340M), and the Department of the Treasury ($267M) are also major buyers, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs ($251M) and the General Services Administration ($236M)."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Accenture Federal Services make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services has $2.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Accenture Federal Services has 375 awards so far this fiscal year and an average award size of $7.5M, and obligations are down 13.2% year over year. Historical obligations include $2.5B in FY21, $2.7B in FY22, $2.9B in FY23, $3.4B in FY24, and $3.2B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from Accenture Federal Services?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense, the Department of Energy, and the Department of Education are the top buyers of Accenture Federal Services in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $486M, the Department of Energy obligated $378M, the Department of Education obligated $340M, and other top buyers include the Department of the Treasury ($267M), the Department of Veterans Affairs ($251M), and the General Services Administration ($236M)."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Accenture Federal Services sell to the government?",
      "a": "Accenture Federal Services sells primarily IT and professional services, led by Computer Systems Design Services. Computer Systems Design Services accounts for $2.5B in obligations in FY2026 to date, followed by Other Computer Related Services ($126M) and Custom Computer Programming Services ($92.6M), with additional work in All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services ($45.2M) and several smaller NAICS categories."
    },
